  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Marlin was originally called Krupp, named after a local German family, but residents requested a change during WWI to avoid anti‑German sentiment.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        24,
        25,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marlin%2C_Washington",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Great Northern Railway built tracks through the area in 1892, and a station named Krupp was erected, sparking the town’s early growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        2,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://kids.kiddle.co/Marlin,_Washington",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1908 the Krupp Signal newspaper began publishing, running until 1917, and a permanent 30‑by‑48‑foot depot opened that same year.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.historylink.org/File/21357",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Marlin’s early economy centered on lumber, with a company operating by 1909, alongside a drugstore, bank, and town band, reflecting a bustling frontier community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.historylink.org/File/21357",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town’s small size makes it Washington’s smallest incorporated place, yet it once hosted a three‑day rock festival in 1971 that was shut down by county authorities.",
